        <mxCell id="uZqQzihwqD7uEiuLLD7T-0" value="In RTKPOST&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;div&gt;The Base position inside Position tab inside options can take different values like:&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;1. Lat/Log/Alt --&amp;gt; Here you can put the values of latitude, longitude, and altitude hard into the respective fields. Which should give the more accurate result, and precise value of coordinates of rover.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;2. From RINEX Header --&amp;gt; Here you might get different and shifted values. As in our case I saw all the coordinates shifted by a fixed value. This could be due to inaccurate coordinate of base station obtained from RINEX Header.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: initial;&quot;&gt;3. From Base Station Position file: I checked this and uploaded Base&#39;s .pos file at appropriate place, but execution is not working, there is some error with reading the pos file i guess.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: initial;&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: initial;&quot;&gt;Below are the shifts I encountered when Base position is obtained from RINEX Header, compared to when hard values are provided to the coordinates of Base station.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;strokeColor=default;dashed=1;align=left;" vertex="1" parent="WIyWlLk6GJQsqaUBKTNV-1">
          <mxGeometry x="77" y="1210" width="703" height="230" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
